<article class="env-lemma" id="07GR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07GR">Lemma <span data-tag="07GR">23.2.6</span>.</a> Let $p$ be a prime number. Let $A$ be a ring, let $I \subset A$ be an ideal, and let $\gamma $ be a divided power structure on $I$. Assume $p$ is nilpotent in $A/I$. Then $I$ is locally nilpotent if and only if $p$ is nilpotent in $A$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $p^ N = 0$ in $A$, then for $x \in I$ we have $x^{pN} = (pN)!\gamma _{pN}(x) = 0$ because $(pN)!$ is divisible by $p^ N$. Conversely, assume $I$ is locally nilpotent. We've also assumed that $p$ is nilpotent in $A/I$, hence $p^ r \in I$ for some $r$, hence $p^ r$ nilpotent, hence $p$ nilpotent. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>